---
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s4">
  <div class="num">04</div>
  <h2>Optional: get Claude to write the request</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    You can describe the thing badly first. That is what the chat is for.
  </p>
  <p>
    For your first build, skip to <a href="#s5">Build your website</a> and use the
    supplied request. Return here when you want to brief an agent on your own project.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open your usual Claude chat and paste the prompt writer below. Answer its
    questions about your project. Describe the result you want, the material you
    already have and how you will check the result.
  </p>
  <p>
    The result covers six things: your goal, context, constraints, what done
    looks like, output format and an optional example.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the result before using it. Remove anything you did not ask for and
    correct assumptions about your photos or personal details. The chat cannot
    see your project folder just because Claude Code can. Tell it what is there;
    do not give it private files to save a sentence of explanation.
  </p>
  <h3>Describe the job, not a list of technologies</h3>
  <p>
    Describe what the site must do, how you want to update it, your budget and
    any existing setup it must fit. Let the agent recommend the simplest
    suitable technology and explain why, then review its recommendation. Astro
    can suit a larger portfolio or blog; React can suit interactive features.
    Neither is a requirement just because you have heard the name. Insist on a
    technology only when it is required, such as for compatibility with an
    existing project.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first exercise deliberately requires plain HTML and CSS so it works
    without more software. For larger sites or an existing portfolio, see
    <a href="/publish/index.html#technology">choosing technology</a> and
    <a href="/publish/index.html#migrate">migrating a site you already have</a>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Prompt writer: paste into Claude chat'}
    code={'I want you to write a prompt for an AI coding agent that works on files on my computer. I am not a programmer.\n\nAsk me questions one at a time, no more than five, about the goal, audience, existing materials, limits and how I will check the result. Do not ask for secrets or private information.\n\nThen write a prompt under 250 words with these headings: Goal, Context, Constraints, What done looks like, Output format, Example. Under Constraints include specific completion checks using only available tools. If a check cannot be performed, tell the agent to stop trying that check, report what remains unverified and explain how I can check it. Do not install tools to complete checks. Unless I name a strict technology requirement, ask the agent to recommend the simplest suitable approach, explain its trade-offs and respect existing project choices. Do not invent missing details. Use plain language. End with "Show me a short plan first and wait for my ok before changing files."\n\nAsk your questions before writing the prompt.'}
    copy
  />
  <p>
    Save this prompt writer in a note for other jobs. When you are happy with
    its answer, paste it into Claude Code with <code class="i">/plan</code>
    before the request. Review the proposed plan before allowing edits. A small correction
    can stay in the existing conversation, also prefixed with <code class="i"
      >/plan</code
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    This approach owes a lot to <a
      href="https://somethingbig.ai/prompting-ai-agents"
      >Matt Shumer's guide to prompting AI agents</a
    >: an agent needs a brief, not a sentence. Give it the context, the limits
    and what counts as done, rather than guessing the code it should write.
  </p>
</div>
